#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188056 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "955fa8ae-4420-51b4-8c17-6ca124656aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "d57db364-7120-581a-a402-af8aaf3b6d92" :question/math-academy-id "e-10420" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.352, \\quad 0.567, \\quad 0.801\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n![](images/2c/2c4ac4b0cf243ba8b49024132642a3856210daa5a603ed534e636caef0083629.svg)\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a <            x <            b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $1 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ 2-y & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{2-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.352,$ we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.352} \\approx 1.214$\n\n- For $y = 0.567,$ we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.567} \\approx 1.396$\n\n- For $y = 0.801,$ we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.801} \\approx 1.668$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188060 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b55868d-99b3-5e04-8505-b7447600eaa0" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "33cf278d-ed9e-5d09-89eb-1076cc7fad66" :question/math-academy-id "e-10425" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.354, \\quad 0.593, \\quad 0.792\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n![](images/ea/ea0ee4d1fcf2e423ebd438e784160e525cb2486e91ba833cd84aae9ff0568344.svg)\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a <            x <            b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we have\n![](images/53/535220c76d363aac546f42314de13394c1fd8b3001e95028a4215867ebc6ed41.svg)\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n![](images/8e/8eef1c7b3d8a69137cf7ac23ecf721751f43449d48b6f450256e15f501ee4c91.svg)\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.354,$ we get $x = \\arcsin(0.354) \\approx 0.362$\n\n- For $y = 0.593,$ we get $x = \\arcsin(0.593) \\approx 0.635$\n\n- For $y = 0.792,$ we get $x = \\arcsin(0.792) \\approx 0.914$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "955fa8ae-4420-51b4-8c17-6ca124656aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c8a159-2ab7-583f-b9ca-e09e33a5305f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.095,\\,-2.423,\\,-1.956" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556533} {:answer/id #uuid "49f3228a-be25-54a2-b493-12bafe2ab3e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.657,\\,-2.134,\\,-2.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556596} {:answer/id #uuid "78ff442a-3704-51bf-96bc-0abf7085c311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.768,\\,-0.773,\\,-0.231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556708} {:answer/id #uuid "8cde8170-c21d-53f8-bce9-7b3b74453420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.189,\\,-0.787,\\,-0.223" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556769} {:answer/id #uuid "e55cf5bc-1685-5922-8164-3a84b98c019b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.998,\\,-1.067,\\,-0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cde8170-c21d-53f8-bce9-7b3b74453420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.189,\\,-0.787,\\,-0.223" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556769} :answer-field/id #uuid "e896c598-93c0-535b-bc5e-efc7006916d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be496a82-fb90-5b74-9be3-d9e0bf10ddb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-147598" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.112,\\,0.455,\\,0.800\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & \\,x\\le 0, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $x\\le 0$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.112,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.112)≈-2.189$\n\n- For $y=0.455,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.455)≈-0.787$\n\n- For $y=0.800,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.800)≈-0.223$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "955fa8ae-4420-51b4-8c17-6ca124656aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b81fbca-a77f-5477-8743-e223ca70c61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.164,\\,0.915,\\,2.717" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556522} {:answer/id #uuid "508e3d35-7d7b-5100-b5d1-8da0c88726c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.983,\\,-2.021,\\,-1.673" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556613} {:answer/id #uuid "88e93c2b-5a1b-5de7-a0d7-7a2abc878221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105,\\,0.876,\\,1.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556752} {:answer/id #uuid "c18dea57-6ee4-588f-8bb8-36b1138d9007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.203,\\,-1.754,\\,0.650" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556896} {:answer/id #uuid "d9721a66-9348-50b8-a97b-da234d7ecc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.802,\\,-0.456,\\,1.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b81fbca-a77f-5477-8743-e223ca70c61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.164,\\,0.915,\\,2.717" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556522} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b52405c-a88b-5363-9895-3267fa25f577" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d55d9f6d-31b7-5824-bc8b-1fe8f38e31d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-147600" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.459,\\,0.714,\\,0.938\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-x}},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve for the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le y\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-x}} \\\\ 1+{e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1-y}{y} \\\\ -x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.459,$ we get $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-0.459}{0.459})≈-0.164$\n\n- For $y=0.714,$ we get $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-0.714}{0.714})≈0.915$\n\n- For $y=0.938,$ we get $x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-0.938}{0.938})≈2.717$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "955fa8ae-4420-51b4-8c17-6ca124656aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c374ab-6913-5cd0-a99e-c528c2de09a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.595,\\,0.212,\\,1.146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556428} {:answer/id #uuid "5992f636-bbef-5d92-8070-1748eaa01d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.093,\\,-0.442,\\,0.856" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556635} {:answer/id #uuid "68357845-bce6-554e-84b7-a271aaeb70aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.372,\\,1.804,\\,2.765" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556675} {:answer/id #uuid "db138c8d-b21c-5164-89b9-248159a62bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.156,\\,-1.045,\\,-0.504" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556975} {:answer/id #uuid "dd78e176-69d9-5cad-a0f6-feffa2678056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.431,\\,1.984,\\,2.598" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00c374ab-6913-5cd0-a99e-c528c2de09a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.595,\\,0.212,\\,1.146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556428} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cabec7-d001-59f7-b4e5-a424a8d1286e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e37507d-c5f2-5123-ae55-8166bf59d6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-147601" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.321,\\,0.567,\\,0.804\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}),\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le y\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{y\\pi }{2} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.321,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.321\\pi }{2}))≈-0.595$\n\n- For $y=0.567,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.567\\pi }{2}))≈0.212$\n\n- For $y=0.804,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.804\\pi }{2}))≈1.146$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "955fa8ae-4420-51b4-8c17-6ca124656aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd7dba3-7e69-5bcf-826b-cc2958b6fd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.203,\\,1.879,\\,2.845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556499} {:answer/id #uuid "649c7229-aa22-5421-af0c-17563057ef06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024,\\,2.132,\\,2.236" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556661} {:answer/id #uuid "6be10170-2653-5422-a7c0-a9ed69479d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.123,\\,2.345,\\,2.934" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556680} {:answer/id #uuid "d7429141-e258-5710-aeba-2d59c02dfe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.134,\\,1.672,\\,2.237" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556960} {:answer/id #uuid "f562c9bf-0f21-562e-89ac-ab5a3185530b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.564,\\,0.045,\\,1.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "649c7229-aa22-5421-af0c-17563057ef06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024,\\,2.132,\\,2.236" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556661} :answer-field/id #uuid "73caafd6-d60a-5362-be7d-d1d46896b046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f583a782-477b-5b0e-85b4-2269efa11788" :question/math-academy-id "q-147602" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.256,\\,0.783,\\,0.809\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<-1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}(x+1), & \\,-1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1) \\\\ 4y & =x+1 \\\\ x & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.256,$ we get $x=4(0.256)-1=0.024$\n\n- For $y=0.783,$ we get $x=4(0.783)-1=2.132$\n\n- For $y=0.809,$ we get $x=4(0.809)-1=2.236$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "955fa8ae-4420-51b4-8c17-6ca124656aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ffc6693-7918-5b27-a0b4-e3a0783bca18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.502,\\,0.798,\\,0.904" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556685} {:answer/id #uuid "8420c9eb-cfa9-54a2-86e8-b718c39b55a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.467,\\,0.623,\\,0.845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556741} {:answer/id #uuid "88f1ede4-d25a-58dc-a83e-6b6a90d5dafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.636,\\,0.793,\\,0.858" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556753} {:answer/id #uuid "942bdd27-dcc3-5986-a885-d194ecacc967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.221,\\,0.467,\\,0.668" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556787} {:answer/id #uuid "e39fbb91-ba93-50b2-be45-d43aa8372324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281,\\,0.546,\\,0.771" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88f1ede4-d25a-58dc-a83e-6b6a90d5dafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.636,\\,0.793,\\,0.858" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556753} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c6038e-d575-5102-98ac-e0a7869bfe80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9e69354-0b9b-54c9-b814-cf1944d62671" :question/math-academy-id "q-147659" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.257,\\,0.498,\\,0.632\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {x}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.257,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.257}≈0.636$\n\n- For $y=0.498,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.498}≈0.793$\n\n- For $y=0.632,$ we get $x=\\sqrt[3]{0.632}≈0.858$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b55868d-99b3-5e04-8505-b7447600eaa0" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.232,\\,1.637,\\,2.284" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556657} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3a9b79-41d8-5dd7-aa99-3bc9dc99d288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.398,\\,1.793,\\,1.942" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556806} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6367be-6630-55f0-be18-4865fbd18d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.920,\\,2.136,\\,2.536" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556925} {:answer/id #uuid "e0af9a98-12d1-5c58-8537-0b157b699025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.343,\\,1.983,\\,2.487" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556984} {:answer/id #uuid "f7321105-ec7d-5b0e-b119-818ca4e002fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.602,\\,1.834,\\,2.673" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "632b77ae-b3d3-5a97-a7ad-12a184e6637d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.232,\\,1.637,\\,2.284" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556657} :answer-field/id #uuid "6506b03f-ca93-59cf-b94c-6064957efb49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ef6d345-db21-5f3a-aad1-2b5e56cc5e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-147698" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.209,\\,0.493,\\,0.826\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $1\\le x\\le e,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & \\,1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $1\\le x\\le e$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ x & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.209,$ we get $x={e}^{0.209}≈1.232$\n\n- For $y=0.493,$ we get $x={e}^{0.493}≈1.637$\n\n- For $y=0.826,$ we get $x={e}^{0.826}≈2.284$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b55868d-99b3-5e04-8505-b7447600eaa0" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ea1ddd-0512-515d-9f5d-135412757c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.503,\\,0.725,\\,0.837" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556574} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.831,\\,0.861,\\,0.916" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556644} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4099-962f-5056-8840-be25081e74c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.035,\\,0.351,\\,0.540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556743} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3874bc-e662-56d9-b74c-96550f2ac2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.404,\\,0.646,\\,0.951" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556763} {:answer/id #uuid "f6672f1f-cc34-5175-9f11-9cf409f90322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.353,\\,0.494,\\,0.631" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec97d5a-b5a2-539b-ab5d-c42e4fe5ae83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.831,\\,0.861,\\,0.916" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556644} :answer-field/id #uuid "f547b9f3-9164-5013-b7a0-bea7e78cacbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "483ad5dd-d09b-5c15-bb9e-2524c193114e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147745" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.691,\\,0.741,\\,0.839\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $0\\le x\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.691,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.691}≈0.831$\n\n- For $y=0.741,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.741}≈0.861$\n\n- For $y=0.839,$ we get $x=\\sqrt{0.839}≈0.916$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b55868d-99b3-5e04-8505-b7447600eaa0" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "653df948-6dc8-5e58-977c-49f0d870e61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.053,\\,2.894,\\,2.931" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556665} {:answer/id #uuid "6567f6f8-c441-5894-846d-a5e89207da3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.034,\\,1.736,\\,1.954" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556666} {:answer/id #uuid "d69c6365-efc5-554a-ac72-1c07eeb313fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.546,\\,3.631,\\,3.778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556954} {:answer/id #uuid "e13b7ccf-e151-5505-959f-cc939906e693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.478,\\,2.253,\\,2.564" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556986} {:answer/id #uuid "f41ac435-1c3a-57c0-b25f-beb895ca90b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.902,\\,1.463,\\,1.683" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d69c6365-efc5-554a-ac72-1c07eeb313fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.546,\\,3.631,\\,3.778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556954} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ee473ff-8143-5b43-81e9-6dad15e3972f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58d37898-ce2e-573d-a33c-7ebee5e8073b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147752" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.405,\\,0.824,\\,0.892\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $0\\le x\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{8}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{t}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<0, \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2} \\\\ 16y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ x & =4\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.405,$ we get $x=4\\sqrt{0.405}≈2.546$\n\n- For $y=0.824,$ we get $x=4\\sqrt{0.824}≈3.631$\n\n- For $y=0.892,$ we get $x=4\\sqrt{0.892}≈3.778$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b55868d-99b3-5e04-8505-b7447600eaa0" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0559a1a5-e628-5eac-99b4-bf34a7beb20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.633,\\,2.784,\\,3.093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556440} {:answer/id #uuid "23f42d23-0ca4-550f-8e95-fa9e2bdb7998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.546,\\,1.699,\\,2.335" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556513} {:answer/id #uuid "856374f3-aa3c-55f9-8008-72655b2648e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.940,\\,2.493,\\,3.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556745} {:answer/id #uuid "992bff68-e6f9-510c-a1a8-204e98c5f359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.443,\\,2.564,\\,3.671" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556799} {:answer/id #uuid "a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.021,\\,1.076,\\,2.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58b4639-5fe8-5f40-b735-09ec616d2904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.021,\\,1.076,\\,2.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556833} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe0e7e2-4434-5ec7-a990-869ba808178d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b352c39a-e73c-5f2e-8ba3-a028566bd47b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147776" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.041,\\,0.137,\\,0.782\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $x\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & \\,x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $x\\ge 1$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =1-y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{1-y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.041,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.041}}≈1.021$\n\n- For $y=0.137,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.137}}≈1.076$\n\n- For $y=0.782,$ we get $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0.782}}≈2.142$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b55868d-99b3-5e04-8505-b7447600eaa0" :knowledge-point/title "Simulating Observations Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed81a42-4f5b-592d-9877-e155aa0820d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.791,\\,1.831,\\,1.983" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556501} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6dcec7-cb37-5574-bdae-e38c0d88cc3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.745,\\,2.323,\\,2.987" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556856} {:answer/id #uuid "d695e024-941b-5f2c-8ffd-481b7c531017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.029,\\,2.322,\\,2.799" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556953} {:answer/id #uuid "d78d7fa8-dcf4-5bb1-99c1-d0866aa1f60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.232,\\,1.330,\\,1.421" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556962} {:answer/id #uuid "eb00bf92-1637-5fdb-aea4-e09fbcb06285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.543,\\,2.806,\\,3.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d78d7fa8-dcf4-5bb1-99c1-d0866aa1f60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.232,\\,1.330,\\,1.421" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556962} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec6b574-4380-5af5-a64d-c3a6fdb29ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a7dadb-337f-50e0-a564-1d8c6c18e0d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147778" :question/problem "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1].$ \n\n$$\n0.198,\\,0.266,\\,0.322\n$$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & \\,1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,{|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & \\,x<1, \\\\ 2(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}), & \\,1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & \\,x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ So, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $1\\le x\\le 4$ and $0<y<1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & =1-\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{2-y}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =\\frac{2}{2-y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{(2-y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.198,$ we get $x=\\frac{4}{(2-0.198)^{2}}≈1.232$\n\n- For $y=0.266,$ we get $x=\\frac{4}{(2-0.266)^{2}}≈1.330$\n\n- For $y=0.322,$ we get $x=\\frac{4}{(2-0.322)^{2}}≈1.421$"}]}
